airbus.detail:
Certification is sequenced, with no test compressed:
- CFM is the EIS anchor;
- P&W GTF2 (ready 2036, 1 year of slack) follows;
- RR UltraFan NB (ready 2037, no slack) goes in only when mature. If it is not ready by EIS, it drops out and NGSA does not wait.

Added to the Board's twice-yearly safety review:
- NGSA certification readiness for 2037;
- the A320 run-down from 82 a month;
- the NGSA ramp to 50 a month by about 2041;
- the 14-a-month tail on the fewest lines.

A quality escape, a safety event or an A320neo engine crunch returns the A320 plan to the Board.

For the 2045 round: a 2045 launch with EIS in 2050, no overlap and $1.0B a year. The case will contain:
- its own grid item;
- the rule-3 check (never after a 787 Re-engine);
- A350F and Spirit A350 absorption shown by a milestone, not a proxy;
- the Re-engine's engine timing. An UltraFan WB launched on our announcement would be ready 6 years later, one year after a 5-year EIS.

There is no public signal. Any announcement is a capital-markets matter for the Board.

cfm.detail:
A cross-functional team runs it, never one person [CX-0012]. The test plan:
(i) harsh-environment dust and hot-section testing at the earliest point in development;
(ii) turn on and turn off: reproduce the LEAP harsh-environment failures in test and show the design removes them;
(iii) yardstick: the CFM56's 7,000-8,000 harsh cycles, not the LEAP's about 4,800, against a company target of 8,000 harsh and 17,000 neutral;
(iv) enhanced inspection of safety-critical parts in new-make and MRO from the first engine.
The engine carries the GE90/GEnx/LEAP hot-section lineage and RISE compact-core technology, not a clean-sheet hot section. A failed gate moves the date, not the gate, and Ali reports any slip to the Board in numbers. 2041 is an internal planning date. Durability is not traded for fuel burn [CX-0208].

Report to the Board if any of these reaches the record:
- an Airbus code change on the NGSA before 2037;
- a P&W-RR Joint Venture commitment (CFM -10 pp NB);
- a Boeing fps launch, its clock and its code;
- a 787 or A350 re-engine, or a Trent 1000 upgrade, that moves the GEnx's 2042 start;
- the LEAP-1B kit's certification outcome. An installed-fleet durability flag there comes ahead of any new programme.

