frame.asks_cfo:
Jay, you're my independent check on estimates [BX-1316]. Run the slip leg as the baseline, not as a sensitivity [BX-0553]. Numbers, please.

1. BUFFER TEST, ROW BY ROW, S4 against a limit of -2.83:
- confirm Do Nothing passes (-0.83) and the Re-engine alone passes (+1.17);
- confirm every fps row fails: C -3.85 (short $1.02B); D -5.18 / -5.47 ($2.35B / $2.64B); E -7.30 ($4.47B); F -15.31 ($12.48B);
- name the rows you would veto.

2. "FUNDABLE WHEN THE 777X TURNS" [BX-0547], from the objective-metrics table, pass or fail for each row. My reading:
- every 10-year row holds stress net debt at $0.0B: pass;
- 7-year at Renton alone holds: pass;
- 7-year at the new site ($0.7B) and with the Re-engine ($2.0-3.1B): fail;
- via Embraer ($1.5-6.3B): fail;
- Re-engine alone: pass.

3. CASH TIMING OF EACH BILL on the brief's standard curve, for a 2035 launch:
- Re-engine: $5B over 2035-40;
- 10-year fps: $60.32B over 2035-45, peak year about 2041;
- 7-year fps: $65.00B over 2035-42.
How much of each falls on surplus cash the Board reserves to itself [BX-0556]? Quote the undiscounted penalties ($19.1B / $22.0B / $52.5B / $0.6B) for scale only; never net them twice.

4. THE 787 RE-ENGINE, the live call:
(a) Your odds of an A350 Re-engine this round, and why. Airbus held in 2030, is funding NGSA "within our financial framework", and calls the A350 its "widebody focus". Doctrine reads Airbus as a cheap re-engineer [B-2446]. Break-even is 55%; it clears epsilon only below about 27%; +0.18 at even odds.
(b) What holding costs. 2045 is the next round (EIS 2050, about x0.37 on the clean-column gain if the structure held), and H3 may close the option first. So a hold is probably the loss of the option, not a deferral. Confirm or correct.
(c) The grid assumes nobody moves after this round, so a later A350 Re-engine would cut the +2.00. How much would you haircut it?
(d) Would you baseline it with buffer, say a 2041 EIS, for "a higher confidence plan" [BX-0548]?
(e) Where your advice sits against the Board's -$1B Downside rule (S3 -1.64).

5. LONG CYCLE (Board recommendation 5). Put the Do Nothing erosion on the record:
- operating profit $0.5-1.4B a year below the status quo from 2048;
- preference 34.0% (2045), 27.7% (2050), 16.0% (2060).
Confirm that the grid compares launching now against never, not against 2045. Tell me what the 2045 package must show beside each fps row.

6. EXPECTED COLUMN. I plan the narrowbody on S1 (S1, S2 and S4 barely differ for non-Re-engine rows), and the Re-engine on S1 blended with S3 at odds p. Tell me if you'd plan differently.

7. PREMIUM LOG. Confirm:
- the fps objective premium would be at least $4.24B, above the cap;
- the Re-engine hold's doctrine premium at your odds;
- the forgone value of a Board-required hold, logged separately from H8.

