frame.asks_cfo:
Rahul, I need the money on one page, with numbers.

(1) Verify the item values to the cent:
- package (B) against hold: +2.04 / -6.00 / +3.20;
- Ducted alone: +0.07 / -6.00 / +0.93;
- Embraer on the Ducted: +1.97 / 0.00 / +2.27;
- Open Fan alone: -21.55 / -10.00 / -23.13;
- Ducted + Open Fan + Embraer + Lobby against B: -21.01 / -9.00 / -22.66.
Also confirm the strain reading: the Ducted alone at -6.00 in S2 is $4B of R&D plus $2B of strain, so the GEnx counts as running.

(2) Weights. Provisionally: S1 0.65, S2 0.15, S3 0.20. S1 is the expected column, and it is the public record, not a hoped-for selection. On these weights the package is +1.07 against hold, Embraer +1.73 and the Ducted alone -0.67. On equal weights the package is -0.25. It breaks even at about 25% on S2 with the rest on S1, or 30% with S1 and S3 even. Airbus's multi-source statement and its request for long-term agreements with CFM weigh against S2, and Boeing's "not yet" against S3. Challenge the weights if the record says otherwise. I'm planning on the low case, so tell me if 15% on S2 is too light.

(3) Pays for itself: is the package at least $1B ahead of hold in S1, with its whole bill charged? And what does it cost in S1 against hold?

(4) Volumes first.
- Ducted: I invoke hard rule 3 as mapped, because the trigger is on the record. NGSA volumes come from Airbus's Alt plan and its public request for engine agreements.
- Embraer: no Embraer airframe or volume is on the record. Does it fail your test? My proposed answer on evidence: no board R&D, no strain added, a worst column of 0.00, doctrine that says it does not need an airframer, and no number or term put out before volumes are agreed. Tell me if that answers your test, or what would.

(5) R&D per year: $4B of R&D plus $2B of strain over 6 years is about $1.0B a year, against about $2.5B. Does the 70% payout stay whole? Is there any rating risk (debt $35B, EV $160B)?

(6) Premium in dollars against the best plan in each column:
- package: $0 in S1 and S3, $6.00B in S2;
- hold: $2.04B in S1 (above the cap) and $3.20B in S3;
- Ducted alone: $1.97B in S1.
Game total so far: $0 of $4.0B, and the RISE allowance is unused.

(7) What the case leaves out:
- an Airbus code change before 2037 (S2);
- a P&W-RR Joint Venture forming (CFM -10 pp NB);
- an fps on a different clock;
- a widebody re-engine moving the GEnx's 2042 start;
- your usual three (a slowdown in airframer delivery schedules, tariff escalation, a global recession).

(8) Terms: any answer to Airbus on long-term A320neo and NGSA agreements carries no price, no concession and no CFM price talk without Safran. Check the draft line for that.

(9) Guide low: give me the expected_pv_b you'd book.

BOARD GUIDANCE (labelled). It vetoes a Ducted alone, the package with S2 expected, and Embraer if your volumes-first test fails it unanswered (the Ducted then falls with it). It wants R&D per year, the 70% payout, any rating risk and every premium stated in dollars.

tests.numbers:
Passes, answered on evidence. No Embraer airframe or volume is on the record. But the board's +5 pp NB is a rule effect of our own order, not a selection, so S1 assumes nothing new, and the item beats hold there (+1.97).
I accept Culp's evidence:
- $0 board R&D;
- $0 added strain (D+E = D = -6.00 in S2);
- a worst column of 0.00, never below hold;
- no capital to gate.
Conditions:
- announce it open and not exclusive, with no volume, price, terms or date;
- keep its $1.97B out of expected_pv_b until an Embraer volume is agreed;
- any real spend (integration, support) comes back to my capex test.
This is doctrine (profile §6), not my record.

Culp's ask (7). The draft line, 'we said we'd commit alongside a committed airframer, and we are', has no year in it. It gives no Ducted ready year, no Embraer start, no Open Fan or RISE date, and does not say 'next generation'. Other moves F(i)-(iv) carry no dates.

Advice for the final text:
(a) keep 2041 and 2042 out;
(b) announce Embraer without a start year;
(c) answer Airbus on long-term agreements with no rate or delivery years;
(d) give no RISE ground-test or flight-test year: my 2022 'by the middle of this decade' fly demo became 'this decade';
(e) keep 'launched and ready by 2050 will not be met' in the rationale and to the Board, not in public. The airframers set RISE's date.

I will re-check every date at the veto check.

The brief has no fuel-burn data. Culp's frame rebuts his own 20% line with my words:
- RISE has 'more than 20% fuel burn advantage';
- 20% is 'practically impossible ... without the open fan. And the reason is physics';
- a ducted engine 'has less than half the fuel burn improvements that can come from an open fan'.

Confirmed: less than half of the open fan's gain cannot meet a 20% line. The Ducted is not a next engine. It is a defensive LEAP successor, in public and in private. The draft line never says 'next generation'.

No Open Fan is proposed. On the record to October 2025:
- over 350 RISE tests with an early focus on durability;
- compact-core HPT dust testing from October 2025, the earliest ever;
- an Open Fan durability owner (Chief Mechanic and Architect);
- open-fan noise below the LEAP's in tests with Airbus;
- ground and flight test 'this decade'. My 2022 'mid-decade' fly demo slipped.

No open durability item was on the record as of October 2025. Ground-test, flight-test and compact-core results after October 2025 are not in my evidence. I report none, and I promise no date.

vetoes.ground:
Physics and test standard (advice):
- never call the Ducted the next generation: it gets 'less than half' the open fan's gain;
- no 2041 and no 2042 in public: we have never turned a ducted product on and turned it off;
- no RISE test year;
- keep 'ready by 2050 will not be met' in the rationale and to the Board, not in the public statement. The airframers set RISE's date.

No safety or durability flag is open on the evidence, and Culp's frame already meets these conditions:
(1) the hot-and-harsh gate is an entry-into-service gate run by a cross-functional team, and a failed gate moves the date, not the gate;
(2) LEAP shop-visit material and time-on-wing kits stay ahead of OE rate and ahead of Ducted development hardware on the 9 constrained suppliers;
(3) any rationing call is cross-functional;
(4) enhanced inspection of safety-critical parts runs from the first engine.

If a gate test fails, I will raise a flag and report the slip in numbers.

other_moves.detail:
A cross-functional team runs it, never one person [CX-0012]. The test plan:
(i) harsh-environment dust and hot-section testing at the earliest point in development;
(ii) turn on and turn off: reproduce the LEAP harsh-environment failures in test and show the design removes them;
(iii) yardstick: the CFM56's 7,000-8,000 harsh cycles, not the LEAP's about 4,800, against a company target of 8,000 harsh and 17,000 neutral;
(iv) enhanced inspection of safety-critical parts in new-make and MRO from the first engine.
The engine carries the GE90/GEnx/LEAP hot-section lineage and RISE compact-core technology, not a clean-sheet hot section. A failed gate moves the date, not the gate, and Ali reports any slip to the Board in numbers. 2041 is an internal planning date. Durability is not traded for fuel burn [CX-0208].

Report to the Board if any of these reaches the record:
- an Airbus code change on the NGSA before 2037;
- a P&W-RR Joint Venture commitment (CFM -10 pp NB);
- a Boeing fps launch, its clock and its code;
- a 787 or A350 re-engine, or a Trent 1000 upgrade, that moves the GEnx's 2042 start;
- the LEAP-1B kit's certification outcome. An installed-fleet durability flag there comes ahead of any new programme.

board_response.response:
Taken up in full. ultrafan_nb_solo: hold. Watson's private other moves set the following:
- test pacing to 2037, uncompressed;
- the durability standard at EIS: a time-on-wing target in the specification, endurance testing complete, life-limited parts at full life;
- a certification-readiness gate in 2036, reported to the Committee, with a public statement if maturity slips. The engine is then not fitted; it is not rushed.
The support plan (partner-shop MRO on the Singapore model, a spares and lease pool, an EIS field team) is carried as live against 2037. The public statement says 'If maturity needs longer, we will say so' and gives no date other than 'ready in 2037'.

recommendations.text:
Hold UltraFan NB and deliver it at maturity. Watson should bring to the Safety, Energy Transition & Tech Committee:
- the test pacing to 2037;
- durability evidence (time on wing, life-limited parts at full life at EIS);
- a gate at which, if maturity slips, we say so publicly rather than compress.
The round-1 support plan is now live and should be reported against the 2037 EIS: partner-shop MRO on the Singapore model, a spares and lease-engine pool, and an EIS field team. P&W has set a public durability standard and Airbus doubts that anyone but CFM will be ready. Our answer is evidence of maturity, never an earlier date.

Capital. McCabe should show:
- the worst column of each item and the remaining Solo spend ($2.29B over 2035-36, about £0.84bn a year);
- the margin-based hurdle case for the triggered Solo that we asked to go to the Audit Committee in round 1 (raised again);
- confirmation that the payout and buyback cadence are untouched.

