airbus.detail:
Toepfer's net-cash path to end-2054 on the all-default package, on his method (an inference), starting from end-2049:
- 30% payout: $134.5B / $145.5B / $156.4B / $166.9B / $177.4B;
- our 48% practice: $88.1B / $95.9B / $103.5B / $111.0B / $118.4B;
- 50% payout: $83.0B / $90.3B / $97.7B / $104.8B / $111.8B.
The payout stays within 30-50%. Any buyback is sized after programme spend, stays inside the 18-month AGM authority (10% of issued capital), and goes to the Board as a separate shareholder-policy item. There is no equity raise, and state support and refundable advances count as upside only. Net cash and the A+/A1 rating are protected. No payout or buyback figures go out in public.

cfm.detail:
FLAG STATUS (Ali, stated explicitly): no safety or durability flag is open. A flag is a finding: a failure turned on in test, or found in the field or in inspection. There is none. Missing data is not a flag, and it is not a pass.
All five items are OPEN, and none closes because 2050 is the last decision year on the board:
(a) the Ducted's harsh and neutral cycles on the fleet leaders (nine years in service), against the CFM56's 7,000-8,000 harsh cycles (not the LEAP's about 4,800) and the company target of 8,000 harsh / 17,000 neutral, with the gate's turn-on, turn-off results;
(b) enhanced-inspection findings on safety-critical parts, in new make and MRO [CX-0010]: none found, and none reported clean;
(c) the LEAP-1B kit's certification outcome, where Ali's last record says early 2026. The MAX is our sole-source slot;
(d) clearance of the 9 suppliers at 15 sites [CX-0613]. The delivery data is consistent with the suppliers carrying the load, but that is not a clearance;
(e) kits and shop-visit material against OE rate, on field data.
In 2035 we told the Board it would have field numbers by 2045. We don't have them in 2050. That's on us. Until the numbers exist, nobody claims in public or in private that the Ducted or the LEAP-1B meets the yardstick. Any failure comes to the Board at once, with the cross-functional answer on evidence. A failed gate moves the date, not the gate. Any durability reserve is booked in the round the need is found.

Report to the Board if any of these reaches the record (Ghai's rough figures; inference):
- an airframer entry date an Open Fan can meet, with a volume. That is the path RISE was built for, and it goes to the Board jointly with Safran;
- a Boeing fps: its clock, its code and any route via Embraer. Any fps launched in 2050 enters service after the 2056 window;
- further MAX decline (10% fewer MAX is about -0.70) and airframer delivery slowdowns (5% fewer NB deliveries is about -0.65, 10% about -1.29);
- a widebody Re-engine or Trent 1000 upgrade undoing the GEnx step (about -0.23);
- any Embraer volume (raises the guide by about $2.1B);
- tariffs or recession;
- the LEAP-1B kit outcome and any installed-fleet durability finding, which rank ahead of everything else.

rolls_royce.detail:
The governance package:
- one engineering interface;
- one durability standard and one fleet report, by operating environment, hot and sandy included, as P&W publishes;
- one chief engineer per change, with each change carrying its own test plan, certification basis and fleet-introduction gate, reported to the Committee;
- crediting as a joint technical valuation recorded at each gate, feeding the share split only: no licence and no cash;
- changes rationed, fleet first.
Any Joint Venture share or term question goes to the Board before it is public. This is company doctrine, not my record [R-0318, R-1010, RX-0072].

Watson reports the Rolls-Royce Joint Venture fleet (about 7,870 engines at end-2049, about 8,848 at end-2050) against the written standard: time on wing in the specification, shop-visit rate, life-limited parts at full life, and aircraft on ground. The brief carries no fleet data, so no figure is invented and neither compliance nor shortfall is claimed. If the data are bad, the order is:
1. operators and Airbus are told first, jointly with P&W;
2. then the public, with the fix and no recovery date we cannot hold;
3. spares and lease engines go to aircraft on ground first;
4. no engineering change is rushed in to cover it [RX-0296, RX-0283, RG-0049].

McCabe confirms the capital frame: no new programme spend (£0), no equity, no new borrowing and leverage unchanged. The payout stays at 30-40%, and buybacks are set on fit, value and timing, never on borrowed money. Narrowbody cash is booked as delivered.
The final lookback, stated plainly:
- $5.71B against the $4B line: missed, beyond repair;
- delivered share 23.6% (2045) and 26.4% (2050);
- IRR about 9.1-12.0% against the 15-18% hurdle: it adds value, but it does not clear the hurdle;
- against the 2035 benchmark (S4, +14.06), a $3.03B engine-value miss.
The IRR is a floor on the board's lifecycle multiple. Joint Venture aftermarket quality (LTSA balance and any onerous-contract test against the written durability standard) becomes a standing Audit Committee item on delivered numbers [RX-0213, R-1579, R-0900, RX-0242].

